WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.606
Begin by framing Foundations of Parallel
Architectures as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:10.125
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:17.404
Topics include the organization of multi-core
computers, parallel computer clusters,

4
00:00:17.404 --> 00:00:24.683
computing grids, client-server systems, and
peer-to-peer systems; computer networks and

5
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:30.506
network protocols; network security;
multi-threaded programming; and network

6
00:00:30.506 --> 00:00:31.234
programming.

7
00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:38.703
Consider this worked case: A multi-core
processor shares a single memory space, while

8
00:00:38.703 --> 00:00:45.597
a distributed cluster connects independent
machines over a network to solve large-scale

9
00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:46.171
problems.

10
00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:53.230
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

11
00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:53.734
conclusion.

12
00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:59.374
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

13
00:00:59.474 --> 00:01:05.860
Now define Distributed-Memory Process
Organization precisely enough that another

14
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.408
learner could test the definition.

15
00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:16.233
Distributed-memory programs must partition
data and work, communicate required

16
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:19.267
dependencies, and avoid deadlock.

17
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:27.707
Consider this worked case: In a MPI program,
each process holds a unique segment of an

18
00:01:27.707 --> 00:01:34.564
array and must explicitly send and receive
data to complete a global computation.

19
00:01:34.564 --> 00:01:41.302
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

20
00:01:41.302 --> 00:01:43.752
evidence would distinguish them.

21
00:01:43.752 --> 00:01:50.252
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

22
00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:58.029
With the foundation in place, examine how MPI
Communication Mechanisms operates in a

23
00:01:58.029 --> 00:01:59.210
concrete sequence.

24
00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:07.147
MPI defines communication among processes
identified by ranks in communicators.

25
00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:14.504
Consider this worked case: A broadcast sends a
single message from one rank to all others,

26
00:02:14.504 --> 00:02:20.022
while a reduction combines values from all
ranks into a single result.

27
00:02:20.022 --> 00:02:27.319
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

28
00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:32.960
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

29
00:02:33.074 --> 00:02:40.015
Next, compare the choices involved in Blocking
and Nonblocking Operations and make the

30
00:02:40.015 --> 00:02:41.617
decision criteria explicit.

31
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:49.227
Blocking and nonblocking operations expose
different completion and overlap behavior.

32
00:02:49.227 --> 00:02:56.848
Consider this worked case: A nonblocking send
allows a process to continue executing

33
00:02:56.848 --> 00:03:02.711
independent tasks while the message is
transmitted, improving pipeline efficiency.

34
00:03:02.711 --> 00:03:10.037
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

35
00:03:10.037 --> 00:03:11.258
the goalposts.

36
00:03:11.258 --> 00:03:18.180
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

37
00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:25.984
Apply Shared-Memory Parallel Programming to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

38
00:03:25.984 --> 00:03:27.083
isolated definition.

39
00:03:27.083 --> 00:03:35.150
OpenMP defines a shared-memory parallel
programming model based on directives, library

40
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.083
routines, and environment variables.

41
00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:45.964
Consider this worked case: A parallel region
directive spawns multiple threads that execute

42
00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:52.633
concurrently within the same address space,
sharing variables unless explicitly marked

43
00:03:52.633 --> 00:03:53.239
otherwise.

44
00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:55.443
private variable scopes.

45
00:03:55.443 --> 00:04:03.191
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

46
00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:03.708
step.

47
00:04:03.708 --> 00:04:08.849
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

48
00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:16.060
Examine the tradeoffs in Worksharing and Data
Sharing, including what improves and what

49
00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.700
becomes more difficult.

50
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:24.126
Data-sharing attributes determine whether
variables are shared, private, firstprivate,

51
00:04:24.126 --> 00:04:26.982
or reduced across threads.

52
00:04:26.982 --> 00:04:34.630
Consider this worked case: A firstprivate
clause copies a variable to each thread,

53
00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:41.101
ensuring independent execution without race
conditions, whereas a shared clause allows

54
00:04:41.101 --> 00:04:42.278
concurrent access.

55
00:04:42.279 --> 00:04:49.028
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

56
00:04:49.028 --> 00:04:56.216
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

57
00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:02.289
Stress-test the reasoning around
Synchronization and Data Races with an edge

58
00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:06.623
case and identify which assumption controls
the outcome.

59
00:05:06.623 --> 00:05:14.003
Barriers and other synchronization constructs
establish ordering where independent execution

60
00:05:14.003 --> 00:05:16.217
is not sufficient.

61
00:05:16.217 --> 00:05:24.354
Consider this worked case: A barrier forces
all threads to wait until every thread reaches

62
00:05:24.354 --> 00:05:29.779
the same point, preventing one thread from
reading uninitialized data.

63
00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:36.358
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

64
00:05:36.358 --> 00:05:43.467
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

65
00:05:43.586 --> 00:05:51.478
Use Performance Analysis Metrics to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

66
00:05:51.478 --> 00:05:52.692
corrective action.

67
00:05:52.692 --> 00:05:58.361
Performance analysis considers communication
volume, latency, load balance,

68
00:05:58.361 --> 00:06:06.864
synchronization, process placement, and the
ratio of useful work to coordination overhead.

69
00:06:06.864 --> 00:06:12.996
Consider this worked case: A poorly
load-balanced cluster wastes resources when

70
00:06:12.996 --> 00:06:19.129
some nodes finish early and idle while others
process remaining work.

71
00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:26.579
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

72
00:06:26.579 --> 00:06:28.176
smallest plausible cause.

73
00:06:28.176 --> 00:06:36.176
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

74
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:42.979
Connect Scalability and Concurrency Evaluation
to the earlier ideas and explain how the

75
00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:45.552
pieces work as one system.

76
00:06:45.552 --> 00:06:52.644
Parallel analysis distinguishes available
concurrency, task granularity, dependencies,

77
00:06:52.644 --> 00:06:58.849
synchronization cost, memory behavior, and
measured scalability.

78
00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:06.051
Consider this worked case: Fine-grained tasks
increase concurrency but raise synchronization

79
00:07:06.051 --> 00:07:12.599
costs, while coarse-grained tasks reduce
overhead but limit available concurrency.

80
00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:22.121
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

81
00:07:22.121 --> 00:07:23.708
across representations.

82
00:07:23.708 --> 00:07:31.036
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

83
00:07:31.154 --> 00:07:38.077
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

84
00:07:38.077 --> 00:07:38.572
analysis.

85
00:07:38.572 --> 00:07:43.744
Incorrectly synchronized accesses can create
data races.

86
00:07:43.744 --> 00:07:51.092
Consider this worked case: A checklist
confirms that MPI ranks are correctly mapped,

87
00:07:51.092 --> 00:07:57.874
OpenMP barriers are placed after shared
variable updates, and load distribution is

88
00:07:57.874 --> 00:08:00.135
balanced across all nodes.

89
00:08:00.135 --> 00:08:08.341
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

90
00:08:08.341 --> 00:08:09.603
verification step.

91
00:08:09.603 --> 00:08:16.283
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

92
00:08:16.283 --> 00:08:16.760
intuition.
